WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.090
Film Courage: If we were going to do a mini
"making of documentary” from LIGHTS OUT

00:00:04.090 --> 00:00:09.010
(the short film version) all until now, what
kind of emotional highs and lows would you

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:10.010
put in that movie?

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:21.420
David F. Sandberg (a.k.a. PonySmasher on Youtube):
Well I mean sort of the lows would be during

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:24.930
LIGHTS OUT I got pretty depressed there for
a while.

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:30.199
When it’s just that shock of “Oh, this
isn't what I thought it was going to be?”

00:00:30.199 --> 00:00:33.390
Or “I didn't think it was going to turn
out this way.”

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:42.410
When you see something but I think on everything
I've done halfway through I get really depressed.

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:43.420
That's true for the shorts.

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:47.530
Even every short that I've made halfway through
I want to quit because I'm like “This is

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:48.570
crap, this is garbage.”

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:53.820
I want to do it and then she [Lotta Losten]
convinces me to go through with it and then

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:56.030
you’re very happy later on.

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:59.820
That’s sort of the thing that has happened
on every movie.

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:01.940
Halfway through you are like “This is bad.

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:04.629
This is not what I wanted.”

00:01:04.629 --> 00:01:10.009
But you have to go through with it and even
with LIGHTS OUT movie the producer told me

00:01:10.009 --> 00:01:17.179
that when you see your first cut, you’re
going to be not very happy (sort of like the

00:01:17.179 --> 00:01:18.810
first assembly of it).

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:24.179
He was saying that when Ben Affleck saw the
first cut of ARGO, he was like “My career

00:01:24.179 --> 00:01:25.179
is over.”

00:01:25.179 --> 00:01:27.840
And then he won an Oscar.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:34.200
Even thought I was told that, the first cut
of LIGHTS OUT movie was “Well this is crap.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:35.200
I’ve ruined my career.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:37.649
I’ve fudged this up, you know.

00:01:37.649 --> 00:01:41.020
Maybe they can cut a cool trailer of it but
the movie is terrible.”

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:44.799
You keep working at it, you cut out all of
those things that that bother you and you

00:01:44.799 --> 00:01:50.950
get the music in there and the DI and then
at the end it’s like “This is actually

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:51.950
pretty cool.”

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:57.309
But on LIGHTS OUT for the post-production
there I was quite depressed and really thought

00:01:57.309 --> 00:02:04.029
it was bad until we had a test screening and
I could hear the audience and feel that energy

00:02:04.029 --> 00:02:05.789
and I’m like “Okay?”

00:02:05.789 --> 00:02:09.629
And then that’s when I was thinking it was
pretty good.

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:18.310
That happens to every movie and also after
every movie wraps you get depressed.

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:24.160
But now after three movies I know myself very
well so I sort of get through it better.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:27.890
When SHAZAM movie was winding down I was talking
to Lotta [Losten] about how I’m going to

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:32.400
get depressed afterwards and it’s going
to be pretty hard but I know it’s coming.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:35.510
Sure enough it does and since you’ve been.

00:02:35.510 --> 00:02:40.739
So many times you are able to ride it out
better because it’s temporary even though

00:02:40.739 --> 00:02:46.520
when you’re depressed it never feels temporary
because depression it tells you that everything

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:52.769
will be crap and everything has been crap
(even the good things are bad).

00:02:52.769 --> 00:03:00.170
So I think that every movie is a rollercoaster
with highs and lows with it.

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:05.129
But I think with just comparing the movies
with ANNABELLE: CREATION movie has been the

00:03:05.129 --> 00:03:08.209
most pleasant one.

00:03:08.209 --> 00:03:13.579
After LIGHTS OUT it felt like Okay, I know
how movies are made here.

00:03:13.579 --> 00:03:17.730
I know the whole process of how it works,
there is nothing unexpected.

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:23.900
ANNABELLE: CREATION movie was sort of a bigger
budget sort of the same thing where it’s

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:26.379
like “Okay, we have more resources, we have
more days.

00:03:26.379 --> 00:03:29.500
We can build sets and a studio.”

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:34.799
And it’s like that was the best experience
so far even though you still have that depression

00:03:34.799 --> 00:03:35.799
and everything.

00:03:35.799 --> 00:03:43.480
And with SHAZAM movie that was such a step
up with such a 100 million dollar budget so

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:49.770
it was kind of back to LIGHTS OUT all of the
sudden like with visual effects and second

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:57.569
units and all of these things I hadn’t done
before so it was kind of difficult at times.

00:03:57.569 --> 00:04:02.010
And then I get depressed and I have to get
through it at times.

00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:08.310
But if I make another 100 million dollar movie
maybe it will be like with ANNABELLE: CREATION

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:14.900
where okay now I know how this process works
and I know what to look out for an all of

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:16.849
these things.

00:04:16.849 --> 00:04:24.120
Film Courage: Do you think that is something
that creators should be aware of that no matter

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:30.740
how proud they feel of something that they’ve
made or a studio film that they will get depressed

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:34.300
because so much time and energy has gone into
it and it’s all over?

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:35.500
David: Oh, every time!

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:37.819
It happens to my brother as well who makes
videos games.

00:04:37.819 --> 00:04:41.360
He spends seven years making a video game
and then it’s done.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:50.310
It’s that thing of…I don’t know if we
think that more is going to change?

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:59.280
Like you work hard like on SHAZAM or whatever
movie and you come out of it and you almost

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:02.460
think like the world is going to be different
now or you are going to be different.

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:03.460
And then….Oh?

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:06.020
It’s back to the same kind of?

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:10.650
It’s sort of a break in routine especially
when you have…like on a movie like this

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:14.890
you have a routine, you have a purpose and
like everyday you come in and you see the

00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:16.460
same people.

00:05:16.460 --> 00:05:20.240
You eat lunch together and talk, you get to
know each other.

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:27.810
It’s like getting fired or someone dying
or something, it’s like “Oh, we are sort

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:33.270
of back to this.”

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:38.210
Things where you don’t change as much as
you expect almost because you are building

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:43.319
to this big thing and then it comes out and
even if it makes money and it’s great and

00:05:43.319 --> 00:05:48.069
people love it but it’s still that feeling
of “Alright…what now?”

00:05:48.069 --> 00:05:59.520
Or you are kind of back to square one in some
ways which is weird but it’s part of life

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:00.520
I guess?

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:07.371
I guess you just have to get to know yourself
better and sort of like yourself and even

00:06:07.371 --> 00:06:13.040
though you did this big thing you are still
the same person.

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:16.190
No big movie or big project is really going
to change that.

00:06:16.190 --> 00:06:18.620
That’s just sort of things around that.

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:23.139
Film Courage: Or you can just start a new
project and go through it all over again?

00:06:23.139 --> 00:06:24.479
David: Yes, that’s the thing.

00:06:24.479 --> 00:06:29.860
I mean right now I’m like I have to get
started on something new.

